In France, voting traditionally takes place without modern technology: voters insulate themselves in the cabin and insert a pre-printed sheet of paper into the envelope, indicating the candidate they have chosen.
After officials verify the identity of the voter, the voter throws the envelope into the urn and signs a list of voters.
France's electoral law contains a fairly strict codification of the course of elections.
Starting in 1988, the ballotacks must be transparent so that both voters and observers can testify that there were no envelopes before the start of the vote, and that no envelopes other than those inserted by counted and authorized voters were added.
Candidates can send representatives, who then witness the whole trial. In the evening, voices are numbered by volunteers under strict supervision and according to specific procedures.
