The secret of their success rests on a system of ecological niches, i.e. that each cat has its own tasks that do not allow it to compete with others.
Lions are the most sociable cats, living in large groups called packs.
The pack consists of one to three relative adult males, along with up to thirty females and cubs.
Females are usually closely related and represent a large family made up of sisters and daughters.
The lion pack behaves similarly to a pack of wolves or dogs, animals surprisingly similar to lions (but not to other big cats) in behavior, and also very deadly for their prey.
The tiger can climb (though not well), swim, jump over long distances and pull with five times more power than a strong person.
The tiger belongs to the same genus (genus panthera) as lions, leopards and jaguars. These four cats are the only ones who know how to roar.
The tiger's roar isn't like the roar of a lion from his full throat, but rather like a sentence of sharp, screaming words.
Steels like to eat small animals. If they have the chance, they catch monkeys, snakes, rodents and birds. Almost all the animals that hunt the ocelot are much smaller than themselves.
