At night they see very well thanks to night vision and are able to move very quietly. They hunt by merging with their surroundings and then jump on their prey.
When a small group (small population) separates itself from the main population from which it originates (for example, when a mountain ridge or river crosses or moves to a new island from which it is not easy to get back), they often find themselves in a different environment than they were before.
This new environment has different resources and different competitors, so the new population will need other characteristics or modes of adaptation to become a stronger competitor than in the past.
The original population has not changed at all, they still need the same adaptations as before.
Over time, as the new population adapts to its new environment, they begin to look less and less like other populations.
Finally, in a few thousand or even millions of years, these two populations will be so different that they can no longer be considered the same species.
We call this process speciations, which means the emergence of new species. Speciation is an inevitable consequence and a very important part of evolution.
