Muhammad was deeply interested in affairs outside the realms of secular life. He often visited a cave known as “Hira” on a mountain called “Noor” (light) and contemplated there.
The cave itself, which has survived for a considerable period of time, gives a very vivid image of Muhammad's clerical focus.
At its location at the top of one of the mountains north of Mecca, this cave is completely isolated from the rest of the world.
In fact, it’s not at all easy to find it, even if it was known that it exists. Inside the cave, it’s in complete isolation.
There is nothing but a beautifully clear sky and a large number of the surrounding mountains. From the inside of the cave you can see and hear very little of this world.
The Great Pyramid of Giza is the only one of the seven wonders that still stands today.
The great pyramid, built by the Egyptians in the 3rd century BC, is one of the many large pyramid-shaped structures built in honor of the deceased pharaoh.
Giza Plateau, or “Gizan Necropolis” in Egypt’s Valley of the Kings, includes several pyramids (of which the Great Pyramid is the largest), small tombs, temples and the Great Sphinx.
The great pyramid was created as a tribute to Pharaoh Khufu, and many smaller pyramids, tombs and temples were built to honor his wives and families.
