The highest Middle Ages was preceded by the early Middle Ages, followed by the Late Ages, which, according to most opinions, ended around 1500.
Technological determinism is a term that, in practice, encompasses a wide range of ideas: for example, so-called technology pusch or technological imperatives, or the belief that human destiny is governed by all-pervading logic, along with scientific laws and their manifestations in technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share two general ideas: that the very development of technology follows a path that greatly transcends cultural or political influence, and that technology, on the other hand, has an “influence” on societies that are inherent rather than socially conditioned.
For example, we can say that when motor cars appear, it will necessarily lead to the development of roads.
However, the national road network is not economically viable for just a handful of cars, so new production methods are being developed to reduce the cost of car ownership.
The mass ownership of cars also leads to an increase in cases of road accidents, causing the invention of new techniques in the health sector on how to repair damaged bodies.
Romanticism carried a considerable element of cultural determinism drawn from writers such as Goethe, Fichte and Schlegel.
In the context of romanticism, geographically shaped individuals, and over time, customs and cultures related to this geography have arisen, and these, because they were consistent with the place of society, were better than arbitrarily enforced laws.
Just as Paris is known as the fashion center of the contemporary world, Constantinople was known as the center of fashion of feudal Europe.
His fame as an epicenter of luxury began around 400 A.D. and lasted until about 1100 A.D.
Its importance declined during the twelfth century, mainly due to the fact that the participants of the Crusades brought back to Europe valuables, such as silk and spices, and they had a higher price at home than those offered by the Byzantine markets.
It was at this time that the title of the capital of fashion from Constantinople was transferred to Paris.
