Self-supplier agriculture is an agriculture carried out to produce only the amount of food that meets the needs of the farmer and his/his family.
Self-supply farming is a simple, often organic system using seed original in a given eco-region combined with crop rotation or other relatively simple techniques to maximise yield.
In history, the majority of farmers have dealt with self-supplier agriculture, which is still happening in many developing countries.
Subcultures bring together like-minded individuals who feel neglected by societal standards and allow them to develop a sense of identity.
Subcultures may be different in terms of age, ethnicity, class, location and/or gender of members.
The characteristics that distinguish subculture can be linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographical, or a combination of factors.
Subculture members often show their membership through the distinctive and symbolic use of style, which includes dressing, behavior and slang.
One of the most common methods used to illustrate the importance of socialization is to draw from several unfortunate cases of children who, due to neglect, accident or deliberate abuse, have not been socialized by adults during growth.
Such children are called “wolves” children. Some wolf children have been imprisoned for a long time (usually they have been imprisoned by their own parents), in some cases they have been abandoned because their parents rejected them because of their severe mental or physical disability.
Wild children may have experienced serious abuse or trauma, after which they were abandoned or fled.
Others are said to have been raised by animals, while some are said to have lived alone in the wild.
In complete upbringing by inhuman animals, a wild child exhibits almost identical behavior (within physical possibilities) as a given caring animal, such as its fear or disregard towards humans.
