Twentieth-century research has shown that there are two areas of genetic variation: hidden and manifested.
Mutations add a new genetic variation, and the selection removes them from a set of expressed variations.
Segregation and recombination shuffle the differences between the two ensembles within each generation.
For primates with a human-like digestive system, it is difficult to satisfy the need for amino acids from plant sources that are available in an open savannah.
Moreover, if they fail to do so, it has serious consequences: the growth of depression, malnutrition, and ultimately death.
Easiestly available plant sources would be proteins available in leaves and legumes, but these are severely digestible for us as primates if they aren't cooked.
In contrast, animal food (ants, termites, eggs) is not only easily digestible, but also provides a high amount of protein that contains all essential amino acids.
Overall, we shouldn't be surprised if our own forefathers have tackled the protein problem in the same way as chimpanzees on the savannah do today.
Interrupting sleep is the process of waking up from normal sleep and falling asleep after a short time (10–60 minutes).
