Domestic goats were mostly kept in herds that roamed hills or other pastures and often guarded by shepherds, often from the ranks of children or adolescents similar to better-known sheep shepherds. These ways of shepherding are still used today.
Horse-drawn railways were built in England as early as the 16th century.
Although stagecoaches mostly consisted only of parallel planks, they allowed towed horses to achieve greater speed and carry heavier loads than on other paths at that time, which were slightly rougher.
Frets were introduced relatively early to keep the tracks in place. However, it was gradually discovered that the tracks are more efficient if they have a strip of iron on their surface.
This has become a common occurrence, but iron has caused more wear and tear on the wooden wheels of wagons.
Finally, the wooden wheels were replaced by irons. In 1767, the first all-iron rails were introduced.
