Virtual teams adhere to the same standards of excellence as conventional teams, but there are subtle differences.
Virtual team members often act as the contact points of their own physical group.
They often have more autonomy than regular team members because their teams can meet according to different time zones, which their local leadership may not understand.
A unique component of the virtual team is also the presence of the so-called “invisible team” (Larson – LaFasto, 1989, p. 109).
“Invisible team” is a management team to which each member is subordinated. An invisible team sets standards for individual members.
