Technology offers solutions in the form of virtual excursions. Students can look at artifacts from the museum, visit an aquarium or admire beautiful art while sitting in the classroom.
Virtual sharing excursions is also a great way to think about a trip and share experiences with future classes.
For example, students from Bennet School in North Carolina create a website every year about their trip to the state’s capital. They rework the site each year, but the old versions remain online and serve as an album.
Even blogs can improve the written speech of students. Even though their blogging is often marked by negligent spelling in the beginning, the influence of other readers usually changes this.
Because students are often the most critical readers, the blog writer tries to improve his writing in order to avoid criticism.
Blogging also “forces students to have an overview of the world around them”. The need to stimulate the audience’s interest inspires students to be smart and interesting (This, 2004).
Blogging is a tool that encourages collaboration and encourages students to expand their education beyond traditional school days.
