The modern city of Casablanca was founded by Berber fishermen in the 10th century BC and was used by Phoenicians, Romans and Merenidas as a strategic port named Anfa.
The Portuguese destroyed the city and rebuilt it under the name Casa Branca, and after an earthquake in 1755 they abandoned it.
The Moroccan sultan converted the city into Dar l-Badya, and the Spanish merchants, who set up their business bases there, gave it the name Casablanca.
Casablanca is one of the least interesting places to shop throughout Morocco.
It's easy to find places around the old Medina where traditional Moroccan goods such as drawer, ceramics, leather goods, hookahs and a wide range of trinkets are sold, but it's all for tourists.
Goma is a tourist town in the Democratic Republic of Congo, located on the eastern outskirts near Rwanda.
In 2002, Goma was destroyed by lava from the Nyiragongo volcano, which buried most of the streets and especially the city center.
Although Goma is relatively safe, any visits outside the city should be well thought out in light of the current state of the fighting that continues to take place in North Kivu province.
The city is also the base for climbing the Nyiragongo volcano and offers one of the cheapest mountain gorilla tracking in Africa.
You can use a body point (motorcycle taxi) for a trip around Goma. The normal price for a short ride is about 500 Congoon francs.
