Sundarbans is the world's largest seashore mangrove belt, stretching 80 km (50 miles) from the coast to the interiors of Bangladesh and India.
Sundarbans has been proclaimed the UNESCO World Cultural Heritage. Part of the forest in Indian territory is called Sundarbans National Park.
However, these forests are not just mangrove swamps, including some of the last remaining stretches of the mighty jungles that once covered the Ganges.
Sundarbans covers an area of 3,50 km2, about a third of which are covered by water bodies or wetlands.
Since 1966, Sundarbans has been a nature reserve and it is estimated that 400 Bengali tigers and 30,000 Indian axises can currently be found in the area.
