Oyapock River Bridge is a suspended bridge. It arches over the Oyapock River and connects the cities of Oiapoque in Brazil and Saint-Georges de l'Oyapock in French Guiana.
These two towers rise to a height of 83 meters, the bridge is 378 meters long and has two lanes with a width of 3.5 meters.
The vertical ground clearance under the bridge is 15 meters. Construction was completed in August 2011, the operation was started only in March 2017.
The bridge is expected to be fully operational in September 2017, when Brazilian customs checkpoints are expected to be completed.
The Guarani tribe was the most important indigenous group inhabiting an area known today as the eastern Paraguay. They lived as semi-Nomadic hunters and also practiced self-supplier agriculture.
The Chaco region was home to other indigenous groups, such as Guaycurú and Payaguá, which made a living hunting, collecting and fishing.
In the 16th century, Paraguay, originally called the “Great Province of India”, was born as a result of a meeting of Spanish conquerors with groups of indigenous peoples.
The Spaniards began a period of colonization that lasted for three centuries.
Since the founding of Asunción in 1537, Paraguay has managed to maintain enough of its original character and identity.
